#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134590} :activity/id #uuid "de69022d-51f9-5731-9e6c-752a541e19de" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428 :topic/id #uuid "484d0a1c-bca0-5a5c-8066-d983f4434a94" :topic/math-academy-id 2174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135180 :tutorial/content "To **square** a number means to multiply that number by itself. \n\nFor example, to \"square $3$\", we need to multiply $3$ by $3.$ We write the \"square of $3$\" as $3^2,$ so we have\n\n$$\n3^2 = 3 \\cdot 3 = {\\color{blue}9}.\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the square of $3$ is $9$\". We could also say \"$3$ squared equals $9$\".\n\nThe number $\\color{blue}2$ in the expression $3^{\\color{blue}2}$ is called the **power** or **exponent**.\n\nThe act of squaring a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $3$ with the following collection of three items:\n\n![](images/4a/4a4dbb46401052829138bddc1653ae843babf4c5cfb6ba9fc40ca29ede99bd70.png)\n\nThen we can picture $3^2$ by creating a square with length and height both equal to $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/cc/cc343eafb463e25ab429bafbc06216dad73037e5a02a2be9adfe046629636013.png)\n\nThere are $9$ items in total in our square, indicating that $3^2 = 9.$\n\nThe first few basic squares are shown below:\n\n| $1^2=1 \\cdot 1={\\color{blue}1}$ | $2^2=2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}4}$ | $3^2=3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}9}$ | $4^2=4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}16}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/ce/ce5db07809eb56891f31ac7fb0257edb42bd650f00c51c25857b02d1333165ad.png) | ![](images/61/6113e94a28fc50c7f1fe77ddf082d28d543aa9e2b6e65f82e8623a89f624f7db.png) | ![](images/cc/cc343eafb463e25ab429bafbc06216dad73037e5a02a2be9adfe046629636013.png) | ![](images/7d/7d56b21746fa8a0053fa32b6c2bc6d9393e13459cb65473e64589cc2f6a01ad3.png) |\n\nThe squares of our usual counting numbers are called **square numbers** or **perfect squares**. We can write them as follows:\n\n$$\n1^2, 2^2, 3^2, 4^2, 5^2, 6^2, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}4}, {\\color{blue}9}, {\\color{blue}16}, {\\color{blue}25}, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "31cd70b9-a231-5c24-b6f4-1a3e714912b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8102 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "532d5ecd-c046-546b-906b-aa64daf1f6f9" :step/math-academy-id 151107 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133507 :question/id #uuid "62c086b3-028f-528b-acd5-7bdc204dcb23" :question/math-academy-id "e-12960"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6f6a3fd-67df-5671-aa5d-08471b2ce1fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443088}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Positive Whole Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "0708b802-2bb7-5823-be5b-b14cb353d3fe" :step/math-academy-id 151108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133508 :question/id #uuid "4a393145-3e63-532b-a288-775fbc7acd15" :question/math-academy-id "e-12961"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eece577d-72b8-5a0d-89fd-1cfa63bf73fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527199}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "70765661-2016-5e64-9ab9-d709f8ecd73b" :step/math-academy-id 151111 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135181 :tutorial/content "The squares of negative numbers are always positive because a negative times a negative is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome squares of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}\\,=\\,(-1)\\cdot (-1) & \\,=\\,1 \\\\ (-2)^{2}\\,=\\,(-2)\\cdot (-2) & \\,=\\,4 \\\\ (-3)^{2}\\,=\\,(-3)\\cdot (-3) & \\,=\\,9 \\\\ (-4)^{2}\\,=\\,(-4)\\cdot (-4) & \\,=\\,16 \\\\ (-5)^{2}\\,=\\,(-5)\\cdot (-5) & \\,=\\,25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** When squaring negative numbers, the parentheses are important! \n\nConsider the following squared quantity:\n\n$$\n-3^2\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as $(-3)^2$. Instead, this is a shorthand way of writing\n\n$$\n(-1) \\cdot 3^2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the order of operations, we should square $3$ *first* and *then* multiply by $-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{3}^{2} & =(-1)\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "38064eee-d6b6-5557-881a-ed03baadeed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8103 :tutorial/title "Squaring Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "4aa26ed8-8e70-561f-a525-88bd5896de45" :step/math-academy-id 151114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133509 :question/id #uuid "55e8797b-62bd-5515-81a5-e1c13249c1cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12962"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e8fb12-e59a-5aba-a9e8-2f872a89b915" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050287}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527154}] :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "fa3f90d0-2a7b-5652-89e0-b78d798e323f" :step/math-academy-id 151115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133510 :question/id #uuid "c07254e9-d6a6-55b1-bab7-4fe453c8cc69" :question/math-academy-id "e-12964"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28f4ffc-5168-5e71-9d77-ae313dd462ad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049194}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527182}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Square of a Decimal"} :step/id #uuid "bf5278f5-4ec6-5286-ae5f-3cbb68840333" :step/math-academy-id 151118}] :activity/title "Squaring Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134034}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506603} :activity/id #uuid "26dc73c3-a573-5ce4-b269-7e050d805860" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428 :topic/id #uuid "484d0a1c-bca0-5a5c-8066-d983f4434a94" :topic/math-academy-id 2174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "552d15ae-c5c8-509e-8ca8-494431dec439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506055} {:answer/id #uuid "68e4b1c8-a132-5e30-a98b-556a99af5dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506088} {:answer/id #uuid "996044d0-4829-58b0-acf3-4fda7d419060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506179} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c01550-6c48-50cb-af06-6cd887f27626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506360} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd4bfdb-d6a7-556d-9522-25b678ff5a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506088} :answer-field/id #uuid "c64d54dd-1bd9-5b4c-bf85-d147344d6818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "14d93e8f-2ff1-562e-a35c-f8de5c3818d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-40857" :question/problem "$(-5.5)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $-5.5$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-5.5)^{2}=(-5.5)\\cdot (-5.5)=(5.5)\\cdot (5.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $55\\times 55=3\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($5.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($5.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $3\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n30\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${5.5}^{2}=30.25.$"} :step/id #uuid "044c8258-6998-590f-acbe-62a40481c0e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f8b7f2-814b-52b7-8406-e6c27493120e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506053} {:answer/id #uuid "586654e7-8734-5135-9af9-664cad8ca414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506061} {:answer/id #uuid "65eac8e3-d47a-5916-a461-305de8d9c4b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506084} {:answer/id #uuid "c7fa432d-1e24-5a86-b227-9bd8e7f5ffaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506265} {:answer/id #uuid "daa57dc6-1f16-5e29-96c2-37eb66dd98de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506061} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa969c6f-c49d-52e8-859b-a1950adae923" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dffd7631-0175-532f-861a-5fc5f04bbe69" :question/math-academy-id "q-42826" :question/problem "$(-10)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To work out $(-10)^{2},$ we multiply $-10$ by itself: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-10)^{2}=(-10)\\cdot (-10)=100\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2eb80d86-6678-5a3e-88d9-4f420dd31528" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e937730-041f-5478-a981-98a01c39c491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132476} {:answer/id #uuid "b12bf7a3-2fd4-50b0-b302-33ed49888285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132477} {:answer/id #uuid "424db066-192b-5a60-b740-a16c46343edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132478} {:answer/id #uuid "6eae60c3-f0ec-5c89-afab-06747c6d0dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132479} {:answer/id #uuid "7f835a56-bce0-51c2-820e-eda856d85dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186132476} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b96da10-424d-55ff-8df7-9396205c5033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30ca79b4-7e41-5f3a-9cf8-5de3bed64d2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42831" :question/problem "$(-15)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Since the product of two negative numbers gives a positive number, we have\n\n$$\n(-15)^{2}=(-15)\\cdot (-15)=15\\cdot 15\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $15\\cdot 15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-15)^{2}={15}^{2}=225.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "56ee66c4-5700-5c25-bdef-89a28b53c5bc"}] :activity/title "Squaring Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505902}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527278} :activity/id #uuid "a5e6c4aa-e33a-5632-9ea3-6d08ffe1753f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428 :topic/id #uuid "484d0a1c-bca0-5a5c-8066-d983f4434a94" :topic/math-academy-id 2174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274904f4-58f7-56e2-9b6d-31c1ae57ce78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526587} {:answer/id #uuid "6431f8e0-22c1-5a92-b706-ab325a5dfa0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526703} {:answer/id #uuid "8776b217-a7c6-5847-98f1-2b8609aea8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{90}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526772} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f1ac2b-edcc-5f11-8220-b6a757312e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526822} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd5b701-3c55-5a13-9705-5e37e7b904b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527012}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527012} :answer-field/id #uuid "695816b9-028c-5cdf-b7e1-17fd412268d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b06c7090-b9ea-594c-b431-3fe9296b347e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99437" :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To work out \n${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2},$\n we multiply \n$\\frac{1}{9}$\n by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{1}{81}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2a01f71d-97d1-5207-b8b2-d2e65ddf34ab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527242}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb37b488-9bf2-5a55-9b98-ff72718291b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526893} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa2a1c0-d3ce-5d21-872c-ede324a37869" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3cfc1cc8-75ac-58cb-baba-115d9b2db8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-185181" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{8}{5}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ (-\\frac{8}{5})\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{8\\cdot 8}{5\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{64}{25} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ca76ade-a0fe-5c1b-bf99-b23c6a48abbb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "540b5a5a-5141-58c1-9bbe-df63004cb07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526674} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d582431-5271-591c-a589-e2a2aea28272" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "056f7c20-573d-55c9-bb54-7bee0a1479fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-185189" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2},$ we multiply $\\frac{5}{3}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{25}{9}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a725bc28-e1db-5604-962d-8da46d9083f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ff1f921-52e3-539a-b46b-8486b70c61bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526596} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef14f82-1314-55e0-aa7f-d6fa02048203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526663} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce61d4c-674e-501c-94f8-21794f4cd123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "53.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526806} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6fe410-d7fb-5fa7-84e2-d245a133c254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526931} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7b2309-a94f-5c47-83f4-fd09d8bb04d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "53.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527009} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b277ddb-c4ed-5fd4-b2dc-c14fccceaffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8c77c325-2e41-51df-821c-943abffd054d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75520" :question/problem "${7.3}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $7.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{7.3}^{2}=(7.3)\\cdot (7.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $73\\times 73=5\\,329.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($7.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($7.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $5\\,329$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n53\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{29}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${7.3}^{2}=53.29\\,.$"} :step/id #uuid "d2bbd8f7-e12b-5835-805d-1d67c253c50f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527241}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2c06f3e-ac54-53ac-853c-0a9d19f1a9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526982}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526982} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f35c7a-0c9b-50f3-b115-b30ce556b18b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9362856b-6dfd-5551-b35f-afef6a56607c" :question/math-academy-id "q-185205" :question/problem "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-4.5)^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $(-4.5)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-4.5)^{2}=(-4.5)\\cdot (-4.5)=(4.5)\\cdot (4.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45\\times 45=2\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($4.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($4.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n20\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-4.5)^{2}=20.25\\,.$"} :step/id #uuid "f52bc408-bc3b-523d-a977-71987b08adcc"}] :activity/title "Squaring Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526492}]}
